# Egress this entry needs — rename `api.acme.invalid` along with everything else.
#
# This file is copied into the deployment's /etc/agent-allowlist when the entry
# is installed. It is the allowlist's own syntax, so what you write here is
# character-for-character what lands there: `domain [METHODS]`, one per line,
# `#` comments, and *.example.com matching on label boundaries.
#
# It exists because your provider does not work without it. The broker file and
# the addon are only two thirds of an install: the proxy denies every
# destination the allowlist does not name, so an entry that ships no egress is
# an entry whose every request fails with a working credential behind it.

api.acme.invalid        GET,POST        # TODO: your host, and the methods it needs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STATE THE METHODS. This is the one line worth reading twice.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Omitting the column is legal and defaults the entry to GET,HEAD,OPTIONS —
# safe reads only. So this:
#
#     api.acme.invalid
#
# is syntactically fine, looks right, and denies every write your provider
# makes. The symptom is every call failing on a provider you have just
# installed, which reads as "my credential is wrong" — and it sends you back
# into the broker file and the addon, which are not where the problem is.
#
# Do not derive this file from the manifest's `hosts` for the same reason:
# `hosts` carries no methods, so copying it produces exactly the line above.
#
# Work out the methods from what your client actually sends. Narrower is not
# safer here — this file governs *reach*, not authority. What bounds a
# credential is its own scope and the addon's host match, neither of which this
# file affects. Narrowing below what the client does buys you nothing except a
# failure that looks like something else.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — anything the provider works without ships commented out.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The default is deny, so a commented line is genuinely off, and turning it on
# is one edit by someone who has just read why they might want it. Put the
# reason on the line.
#
# telemetry.acme.invalid  POST    # usage reporting; the client works without it

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is NOT the manifest's `hosts`, and the difference matters.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `hosts` is where the addon ATTACHES A CREDENTIAL. This file is where the lab
# MAY SEND A REQUEST. They overlap, and they are not the same list:
#
#   - Every host in `hosts` must appear UNCOMMENTED here, or the addon injects
#     into requests the proxy denies — a credential minted, audited as issued,
#     and never spent. 00-config-lint fails on that.
#   - The reverse does not hold, deliberately. A host may belong here and never
#     in `hosts`: an error-reporting endpoint, a git remote, anything on a
#     multi-tenant suffix. Attaching your credential to one of those is the
#     accident the addon's host matching exists to prevent.
#
# See bank/github/allowlist (github.com is here and not in hosts) and
# bank/cloudflare/allowlist (*.workers.dev is fine to reach, a bug to inject
# for) for the two cases worth understanding before you write your own.
